# script-generation

## main purpose

The purpose of this script is to transform a text (e.g. a short story) into a script that can be used by the text-to-voice functionality.

The input is a simple text file, the output is a JSON with the following structure:

```
{
    "voices": [
        "voice_name": {
            "voice": "name of the openai voice",
            "instructions": "instructions for the openai TTS endpoint"
        }
    ],
    "fragments": [
        {
            "voice_name": "voice",
            "text": "text",
            "instructions": "optional additional instructions for the voice endpoint"
        }
    ]
}
```

the input script can have one or more voices, and one or more fragments. 

Each voice has default instructions, that 

Examples of voices are:
- narrator
- protagonist
- antagonist

Each fragment can contain instructions that, if present, will replace the default instructions.

The main purpose of this project is to automatically produce audiofles for each fragment, calling OpenAI TTS endpoints. 

## structured output

use openai structured output to ensure the format of the generated script follows the expected schema.

## text split into fragments

the input text is a single string. The script-generator understands how to split the text into a series of fragments. Each fragment uses a single voice. For example in a conversation each sentence will be a a different fragment, because each sentence is spoken by a different person.

This task is performed by an LLM, using OpenAI endpoints.

## verification

the generated script is checked for completeness.

## instructions for voices

each fragment might require a specific intonation to convey the correct emotion. This is done by using the "instructions" field to describe the emotion of the character that is speaking (if required). 

# text-to-voice

## input data

the input/scripts folder contains a json with the following structure

the JSON is described in the script-generation feature


## output data

the `output\fragments` folder will hold the generated audio files. Each fragment in the input JSON will have a corresponding audio file. The filenames of the generated audiofiles will have this format `fragment_000.wav`, composed of the `fragment_` prefix and the index of the fragment in the array of fragments. Fragments will be created following the order of the array in the input JSON.

## automatic splitting of long fragments

the length of fragments is checked, and if a fragment is too long it will be split in multiple fragments, each of a length less than the maximum allowed by OpenAI TTS endpoints. The generated audiofiles will have this format `fragment_xxx_yyy.wav` where `xxx` is the index of the fragment, and `yyy` is the index within the splits of the fragments.

## rate limits

OpenAI imposes rate limmits that will be respected by slowing down the execution, if necessary

## parallel execution

the script work in a single thread, to make it easier to check rate limit and check progress

## error handling

if a fragment produced an error, it will be printed in the console log, and the generation will be attempted again, to a maximum of 3 attempts. 

A list of fragments that could not be transformed in audio files will be generated and will be put in the `output` folder in a file called `errors.json`.

## avoid processing fragments twice

if the audio file of a fragment is already present in the output folder, skip it

## log

the script will generate a log of operations and will be written in the `output` folder in a file called `log.json`

# audio-merge

## main purpose of this functionality

the `output\fragments` folder contains a list of audio files that follow this format `fragment_xxx.wav` or `fragment_xxx_yyy.wav`. The goal is to merge all the fragments into a single output audio. The fragments are aggregated keeping the order indicated by the file name. Only fragment audio files are aggregated (file name starts with `fragment_`). The generated audio is created in the `output/mixdown` folder with the name `mixdown.wav`.

## normalisation

the output audio file is ormalised at -3 Db.

## padding

the output audio file has a few seconds of silence at the beginning and at the end

## logging

the file appends logs to the logfile

## error handling

if an error occours, it's printed to the console.

